    "bounding box" : [ 133.798, 585.509, 477.528, 625.352 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "By Theorem 12, it follows that there is an eub h for f such that cf(h(i)) > λ for all i < ω. Claim 14. cf(h(i)) ≥ λ+3 for all but ﬁnitely many i < ω."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.798, 502.268, 477.598, 576.611 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof. If not, then there exist k ∈ {1,2} and an unbounded A ⊆ ω such that, for all i ∈ A, we have cf(h(i)) = λ+k. For each i ∈ A, let {δηi : η < λ+k} enumerate, in increasing fashion, a set of ordinals coﬁnal in h(i). For each η < λ+k, deﬁne a function hη from ω to the ordinals by letting hη(i) = δηi if i ∈ A and hη(i) = 0 otherwise. For each η < λ+k, we have hη <∗ h, so, since h is an eub for f, there is βη < λ+3 such that hη <∗ fβ"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.798, 452.19, 477.538, 514.03 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : ". Let γ = sup{βη : η < λ+k}. Since k < 3, we have γ < λ+3. Therefore, for all η < λ+k, we have hη <∗ fγ. Fix an unbounded B ⊆ λ+k and an n < ω such that, for all η ∈ B, we have hη <n fγ. But then, for all i ∈ A \\ n, we must have fγ(i) ≥ sup{δηi : η ∈ B} = h(i), contradicting the fact that h is an upper bound for f."
  }, {
